course: dictionary
lesson: 13171
title: Fractional: parts and tiny amounts
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Fractional has two meanings. It can mean to do with fractions, like 1/2 or 3/4. It can also mean very small. The words around it show you which meaning is right.
explain: «The race was won by a fractional difference: a hundredth of a second.» A hundredth of a second is tiny, so fractional here means very small. Check it by swapping in very small: «won by a very small difference» still makes sense.
explain: «The recipe needs fractional parts of a cup, such as half and a quarter.» Here the clue is half and a quarter, which are fractions. So fractional means to do with fractions, or parts of a whole.
fact: Fractional means to do with fractions, like 1/2 or 3/4.
fact: Fractional also means very small: «a fractional increase» is only a tiny rise.
fact: «Fractional parts» are the parts of a whole, such as halves and quarters.
fact: «A fractional difference» is a very tiny gap, like a hundredth of a second in a race.
fact: Fractional answers are not whole numbers.
exercise: «The shop raised its prices by a fractional amount, only a few pence.» What does fractional mean here? | *Very small | Made of sugar | Very large | Happening at night
exercise: «Half and a quarter are fractional amounts.» What are fractional amounts? | Long walks in the park | Whole numbers like 5 and 10 | *Parts of a whole, like 1/2 or 3/4 | Kinds of music
exercise: Ben says a fractional answer is always a whole number. Is he right? | Yes, fractional always means whole | Yes, because fractions are big | *No, a fractional answer is not a whole number | No, because fractions are sweets
test: Which of these is a fractional number? | *Two-thirds, written 2/3 | Ten pounds | Twelve | Seven apples
test: «The team won by a fractional margin.» Does this mean the win was big or tiny? | Late at night | Huge, by many goals | *Tiny, by a very small amount | Loud and noisy
exam: Which sentence uses fractional to mean very small? | «The fractional team sang at the party.» | «The cake was cut into fractional parts.» | *«The rise was only a fractional increase, a few pence.» | «The map showed fractional numbers on the lines.»
